<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  ENG-21: hardened-runtime entitlements for the signed macOS .app.

  Deliberately one key. The hardened runtime itself (`codesign --options
  runtime`, which PyInstaller enables whenever a signing identity is passed) is
  mandatory for notarization; the entitlements usually pasted into Python-bundle
  guides are not. Each omission was tested rather than assumed — see
  `plans/eng-21-signing-notarization.md` → Entitlements:

    * allow-unsigned-executable-memory — omitted. Plain mmap(PROT_WRITE|
      PROT_EXEC) without MAP_JIT fails with EPERM on Apple silicon even *with*
      the entitlement granted; it only ever did anything on x86_64. The
      PyInstaller ctypes MemoryError it is cargo-culted from (#4629) was an
      Intel-era bug in old libffi.
    * disable-library-validation — omitted. It papers over dyld's "mapping
      process and mapped file (non-platform) have different Team IDs", i.e.
      nested binaries that do not carry our Team ID. Signing all of them is the
      real fix, and PyInstaller's inside-out signing does exactly that. Kaleido's
      downloaded Chrome is a separate *process*, not a loaded library, so library
      validation does not apply to it.
    * com.apple.security.network.* — omitted. App Sandbox keys; a non-sandboxed
      Developer ID app ignores them, and the Application Firewall does not prompt
      for loopback listeners.

  allow-jit is kept only as cheap insurance against a libffi built without static
  trampolines (libffi >= 3.4.2 uses them and needs no JIT entitlement at all).

  If a notarization log or a runtime crash proves one of the omitted keys is
  needed, add it *with that log line as the justification* — not pre-emptively.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
